Unexpected deployment targets|A playbook resolves more hosts than the operator intended.|ansible-playbook -i inventory.yml site.yml --list-hosts|Inspect group membership, inventory plugins and host-pattern expansion. Confirm limits apply to every relevant play.|Stop before execution and correct inventory filters or the host pattern. Use a verified canary subset before broader batches.|Repeat target listing and confirm exact expected hosts, then verify no unrelated hosts changed after rollout.
Every run reports changes|A configuration play reports changed on every execution despite stable desired state.|ansible-playbook -i inventory.yml site.yml --check --diff --limit CANARY|Identify tasks with nondeterministic template values or shell commands lacking state-aware conditions. Distinguish reporting errors from actual repeated writes.|Use purpose-built modules, stable templates and accurate changed_when rules. Do not mark real changes unchanged merely to make the recap green.|Run twice on a test host; the second run should show no unintended changes or restarts while deliberate drift is still corrected.
Handler never restarts service|A configuration file changes but the service keeps old behavior.|ansible-playbook -i inventory.yml site.yml --list-tasks|Check notify names, task changed status, handler scope and earlier failures. A handler may not run after a later failure under default behavior.|Correct notification wiring and validate configuration before restart. Choose failure/handler behavior deliberately rather than forcing every handler unconditionally.|Change a harmless test setting and confirm one restart or reload occurs and the application uses the new configuration.
Check mode misses a failure|A dry run passes but the real deployment fails on a dependent task.|ansible-doc MODULE_FQCN|Review module check-mode support and tasks that depend on registered results or resources not created during check mode.|Add representative integration tests and explicit validation for the dependency. Treat check mode as one signal rather than the sole gate.|Run the real workflow on an isolated canary and test both the first and second execution before promotion.
Secret appears in output|A failed template task prints a credential into the CI log.|ansible-playbook -i inventory.yml site.yml --list-tasks|Trace where the secret entered task arguments, diffs, debug output or loop labels. Identify log readers and retained artifacts.|Rotate the exposed value, restrict affected logs and add no_log or diff suppression where needed. Secure generated files as well as controller output.|Use a nonproduction sentinel secret in a controlled test and confirm it does not appear in logs or artifacts.
Variable unexpectedly overridden|A role uses a production endpoint during a staging run.|ansible-inventory -i inventory.yml --host HOST|Compare inventory variables with role defaults, play vars and runtime extra variables. Inventory output alone does not show every precedence source.|Remove ambiguous overrides and make environment inputs explicit. Add assertions for expected environment and endpoint before mutation.|Render and validate configuration for each environment and verify the effective endpoint on the canary.
Rolling update removes too much capacity|A service becomes unavailable during a fleet play.|ansible-playbook -i inventory.yml site.yml --list-hosts|Inspect serial, forks, strategy and health checks. Confirm the batch size relative to replica count and failure domains.|Stop expansion, restore healthy capacity and reduce batch size. Gate each batch on application readiness and dependency capacity.|Run a controlled rollout and verify minimum serving capacity and user success remain within the objective throughout.
Privilege escalation fails|SSH connectivity works but privileged tasks fail.|ansible -i inventory.yml CANARY -m ansible.builtin.ping|Separate connection identity from become identity and inspect the actual sudo policy and error. Connectivity success does not prove escalation rights.|Grant the narrowly required escalation or adjust task scope to avoid unnecessary privilege. Do not default every role to unrestricted root.|Test the required privileged operation and confirm unrelated privileged operations remain denied.
Run-once migration repeats|A database migration runs more than once during a serial deployment.|ansible-playbook -i inventory.yml site.yml --list-tasks|Check whether run_once is evaluated once per serial batch and whether delegation causes shared-target concurrency.|Move the migration to an explicitly coordinated stage or dedicated play and make it idempotent. Protect it with an application-level migration lock where appropriate.|Test multiple batches and verify exactly the intended migration outcome with safe handling of retries.
Partial configuration after failure|A play changes several files and then fails before validation completes.|ansible-playbook -i inventory.yml site.yml --check --diff --limit CANARY|Inspect which tasks completed and actual target state. Rescue behavior is not a transaction rollback, and unreachable hosts need separate handling.|Restore a known-good configuration or complete a controlled forward repair, then improve validation-before-activation and recovery blocks.|Verify application health and a clean repeat run on affected hosts before continuing the fleet.
